WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.946
- I love the allure, the danger,
of working without a net.

00:00:04.946 --> 00:00:08.777
Somebody goes to work an actually makes

00:00:08.777 --> 00:00:13.133
a Daffy Duck cartoon, and I
thought, I want to do that.

00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:14.708
My mom would say things
like, well, yes honey,

00:00:14.708 --> 00:00:17.188
that is a very good Elmer Fudd impression,

00:00:17.188 --> 00:00:19.788
but it's not going to
help you later in life

00:00:19.788 --> 00:00:21.538
with your profession.

00:00:22.481 --> 00:00:24.042
(Elmer Fudd laugh)

00:00:24.042 --> 00:00:25.618
you know what, I'm going to go in there

00:00:25.618 --> 00:00:27.602
and give the best damn
joker they've ever heard

00:00:27.602 --> 00:00:29.900
and they're going to really
regret the fact that they can't

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.241
hire me

00:00:31.241 --> 00:00:32.074
(swoosh)

00:00:32.074 --> 00:00:34.146
- What's up, believe nation? It's Evan!

00:00:34.146 --> 00:00:36.424
My one word is believe, and I believe

00:00:36.424 --> 00:00:37.469
that you can do something special that can

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:39.391
help change the planet.

00:00:39.391 --> 00:00:41.185
So to help you on your journey
today we're going to learn

00:00:41.185 --> 00:00:42.610
from actor Mark Hamill and my take

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.619
on his top ten rules for success

00:00:45.619 --> 00:00:46.748
(swooshing)

00:00:46.748 --> 00:00:49.331
(upbeat music)

00:00:58.731 --> 00:01:02.588
- As a kid I was just mad
about the original King Kong

00:01:02.588 --> 00:01:04.940
I loved all the stop-frame
animation, Ray Harryhausen

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.773
pictures, and horror, and fantasy,

00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:10.822
you know I loved all the black and white

00:01:10.822 --> 00:01:14.312
universal films, I still
do. When I realized,

00:01:14.312 --> 00:01:18.479
I think I saw a documentary
on the Disneyland show,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.964
which it was called then,
where you saw Clarence Nash

00:01:22.964 --> 00:01:26.652
recording the voice of
Donald Duck. I don't know

00:01:26.652 --> 00:01:29.378
if I was six, seven, but
it suddenly occurred to me,

00:01:29.378 --> 00:01:31.961
these things don't just emerge full-blown,

00:01:31.961 --> 00:01:36.433
somebody goes to work and
actually makes a Daffy Duck

00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:39.348
cartoon, and I thought,
"I want to do that!"

00:01:39.348 --> 00:01:41.333
I mean, I wasn't sure I
wanted to be an actor,

00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:44.276
I thought I could, I didn't
know what I wanted to be

00:01:44.276 --> 00:01:46.631
but I certainly wanted
to be in the business

00:01:46.631 --> 00:01:50.955
of going to work and having a colossal ape

00:01:50.955 --> 00:01:53.172
carry a woman up the Empire State Building

00:01:53.172 --> 00:01:55.928
I thought, you know, it was
just mind-boggling to me

00:01:55.928 --> 00:02:00.095
you know, that somebody got to
do that for their profession

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.025
I don't have a lot of
people coming up and saying

00:02:04.025 --> 00:02:06.839
you know, I should be on
Broadway, I do this and that,

00:02:06.839 --> 00:02:08.311
or I should be in movies, or whatever,

00:02:08.311 --> 00:02:10.399
but lots of people say
oh, I could do cartoons.

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:12.781
Listen to my Homer
Simpson, well that IS good,

00:02:12.781 --> 00:02:14.320
I like that.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:15.198
(audience laughs)

00:02:15.198 --> 00:02:17.696
But it's, you got to
be dedicated, you know,

00:02:17.696 --> 00:02:20.726
it's not a matter of being able
to do a good Homer Simpson,

00:02:20.726 --> 00:02:23.536
and you're going to have
a career, it's hard work,

00:02:23.536 --> 00:02:26.231
I go to drama classes all
the time and I hate to be a

00:02:26.231 --> 00:02:30.307
buzz kill, but I try and talk
kids out of showbusiness,

00:02:30.307 --> 00:02:31.894
(audience laughs)

00:02:31.894 --> 00:02:33.855
Seriously, I say if
there's anything you like

00:02:33.855 --> 00:02:36.953
as much as performing,
even if you get into it

00:02:36.953 --> 00:02:40.692
academically and become a
drama teacher, or if you,

00:02:40.692 --> 00:02:45.532
if there's something else
you like, you can still do

00:02:45.532 --> 00:02:49.137
community theater, you can
do amateur theater, you can

00:02:49.137 --> 00:02:53.625
still perform, but I'm telling
you, if you get into it

00:02:53.625 --> 00:02:57.724
as a profession, you're in
for a lifetime of rejection,

00:02:57.724 --> 00:03:01.891
and er, erm, unemployment,
I mean, you could have,

00:03:03.922 --> 00:03:06.513
you get seven or eight jobs
and then you don't work

00:03:06.513 --> 00:03:09.418
for a year and a half, you
know? And you start running out

00:03:09.418 --> 00:03:13.573
of money, you have to go
drive a cab, or wait tables,

00:03:13.573 --> 00:03:17.192
or something. It's a miserable
profession. Believe me.

00:03:17.192 --> 00:03:19.176
Please don't do it.

00:03:19.176 --> 00:03:21.142
(audience laughs)

00:03:21.142 --> 00:03:23.690
And that's not just from
the standpoint that,

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:26.231
you know, obviously there's
probably people in the audience

00:03:26.231 --> 00:03:29.156
more talented than me that
will be stealing my jobs

00:03:29.156 --> 00:03:32.934
if they succeed, it's not
that, it's just that it is,

00:03:32.934 --> 00:03:36.052
only through the, if you
can't talk someone out of

00:03:36.052 --> 00:03:38.965
show business, then they have a shot.

00:03:38.965 --> 00:03:43.196
I love the allure, the danger
of working without a net.

00:03:43.196 --> 00:03:46.679
You know, it's one thing
to be a tightrope walker,

00:03:46.679 --> 00:03:50.117
if there's a net. As opposed
to being a tightrope walker

00:03:50.117 --> 00:03:53.478
where you could plummet to
your death at any moment,

00:03:53.478 --> 00:03:57.645
and that's the disconnect
between someone who's rational

00:04:00.169 --> 00:04:04.170
and someone who's just crazy
enough to go into show business

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.187
and stick to it no matter
what. Tenacity is almost

00:04:06.187 --> 00:04:10.670
more important than talent.
It's equally as important,

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:11.503
for sure.

00:04:12.394 --> 00:04:16.522
I really love what I do. I'm
so lucky to be able to do

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:19.464
what I wanted to do when
I was a kid, you know,

00:04:19.464 --> 00:04:21.375
I had a little tape recorder,
I would tape, you know,

00:04:21.375 --> 00:04:23.362
I loved the sound of peoples' accents,

00:04:23.362 --> 00:04:27.332
Bella Lugosi, (does Bella Lugosi
accent) I never dreamed why

00:04:27.332 --> 00:04:30.690
I thought where's this guy
from, why's he talk like that?

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:34.857
Hearing the Beatles' dialects.
I loved the sound of it all.

00:04:36.301 --> 00:04:40.217
And so I would be practicing
and my mom would say things

00:04:40.217 --> 00:04:43.125
like well, yes honey, that
is a very good Elmer Fudd

00:04:43.125 --> 00:04:46.384
impression but it's not going
to help you later in life

00:04:46.384 --> 00:04:50.314
with your profession.
(Does Elmer Fudd laugh)

00:04:50.314 --> 00:04:55.169
I haven't played, no, I
haven't played Elmer Fudd,

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:59.336
but I loved Peter Laurie, I
love Boris Karloff, I loved

00:05:01.019 --> 00:05:04.691
the way he spoke, (does
impression) I don't know where

00:05:04.691 --> 00:05:06.725
he's from, and he's got a slight lisp,

00:05:06.725 --> 00:05:10.892
but I loved the sound. Laurel
and Hardy, I said where's this

00:05:11.807 --> 00:05:15.411
guy from? Before the Internet,
I know a lot of you can't

00:05:15.411 --> 00:05:17.544
remember this, but before
the Internet you actually had

00:05:17.544 --> 00:05:20.239
to go to the library and
look things up in books.

00:05:20.239 --> 00:05:23.061
It was very embarrassing
cos I'm not really an

00:05:23.061 --> 00:05:25.339
intellectual person, I don't
want to know about Russian

00:05:25.339 --> 00:05:29.506
history, and ancient Rome,
I said, you know, do you

00:05:30.647 --> 00:05:33.457
have any books on the Three
Stooges, you know (laughs)

00:05:33.457 --> 00:05:35.471
But you know I like having fun.

00:05:35.471 --> 00:05:39.265
- When you see Mark become
the Joker, when he's actually

00:05:39.265 --> 00:05:41.764
in the recording booth, and
he's doing these voices,

00:05:41.764 --> 00:05:43.912
he actually physically
kind of twists himself

00:05:43.912 --> 00:05:46.028
and manifests himself into joker.

00:05:46.028 --> 00:05:47.615
(Mark laughs like the Joker)

00:05:47.615 --> 00:05:50.259
- Mark sometimes thinks he
actually is Joker, so he

00:05:50.259 --> 00:05:52.391
just turns up and he just nails it.

00:05:52.391 --> 00:05:54.279
In fact we wrote all the
dialog to really fit into where

00:05:54.279 --> 00:05:56.173
her was taking Joker.

00:05:56.173 --> 00:05:57.508
- Surprise! (laughs)

00:05:57.508 --> 00:05:59.892
(Batman groans)

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:03.642
- You fell for the old
sick Joker gag, Batman

00:06:05.724 --> 00:06:08.022
(female character grunts)

00:06:08.022 --> 00:06:09.284
(noise of hitting the ground)

00:06:09.284 --> 00:06:10.457
- [Mark] One of the things
that informs the Joker

00:06:10.457 --> 00:06:12.077
is his laugh. I remember
reading the comic books

00:06:12.077 --> 00:06:15.149
it was ha ha ha ha ha ha ha,
but ho ho ho hee hee, hoho hee.

00:06:15.149 --> 00:06:17.604
(Joker laugh)

00:06:17.604 --> 00:06:20.070
- His laugh should be
like a musical instrument

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.933
it should sort of illustrate his mood

00:06:22.933 --> 00:06:26.743
it could be ominous and intimidating,

00:06:26.743 --> 00:06:29.021
(intimidating joker laugh)

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:32.891
- It could be gleeful and wild abandon

00:06:32.891 --> 00:06:35.869
(gleeful joker laugh)

00:06:35.869 --> 00:06:38.837
- But I didn't want to
just have one rote laugh

00:06:38.837 --> 00:06:42.123
(another joker laugh)

00:06:42.123 --> 00:06:44.621
- I was driving from the first
recording session, thinking

00:06:44.621 --> 00:06:48.788
now how did I laugh? I'm on
the freeways in Los Angeles

00:06:49.996 --> 00:06:52.795
(laughs) no that's not it
(laughs) no that's not it

00:06:52.795 --> 00:06:57.216
Now I'm sure no one in Los
Angeles would look twice at

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:59.661
somebody, look at that guy
laughing maniacally, in his car

00:06:59.661 --> 00:07:01.699
(Joker laughs)

00:07:01.699 --> 00:07:03.757
- It's actually really fun to watch him

00:07:03.757 --> 00:07:06.910
perform the character
because all the other actors

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.570
are all seated and Mark
has to stand to do it,

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:12.744
and he gets his whole body into it.

00:07:12.744 --> 00:07:15.187
- They used to kid me
because I was the only one

00:07:15.187 --> 00:07:16.451
who would stand up

00:07:16.451 --> 00:07:19.041
- [Joker] Yes well, this
is all tremendously boring

00:07:19.041 --> 00:07:21.040
- They had these little
you know, those separators

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.857
that you see for musicians,
so your sound doesn't

00:07:23.857 --> 00:07:25.602
bleed into other peoples'
mics. And everybody's

00:07:25.602 --> 00:07:27.717
sitting down at their little music stand,

00:07:27.717 --> 00:07:30.423
and I just thought to
energize the character,

00:07:30.423 --> 00:07:32.670
to shake my fists, and I
felt I've got to stand up.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:36.337
One thing that gave me
confidence is I said,

00:07:37.348 --> 00:07:39.618
there's no way they're
going to cast the guy

00:07:39.618 --> 00:07:43.678
who plays Luke Skywalker
to be this icon of villainy

00:07:43.678 --> 00:07:46.427
so I was really cocky
in the sense that I knew

00:07:46.427 --> 00:07:49.250
I couldn't get the part.
Sometimes what trips you up

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:52.970
is that you want it so
bad your nerves betray you

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:56.303
and your timing's off. You over-sell it.

00:07:57.409 --> 00:07:59.586
Or I don't know, it's hard to describe.

00:07:59.586 --> 00:08:02.027
But the one thing that
I felt about Joker was,

00:08:02.027 --> 00:08:04.776
since there's no way I can
get this, just from the

00:08:04.776 --> 00:08:09.743
standpoint of, of, the,
publicity of saying, you know,

00:08:09.743 --> 00:08:12.951
cos, years after I did it,
people kept saying, that Mark

00:08:12.951 --> 00:08:17.859
Hamill? You know, and
they'd demand on the street,

00:08:17.859 --> 00:08:20.547
do the voice...that's not you, is it?!

00:08:20.547 --> 00:08:23.880
But, er, I had the confidence of knowing

00:08:25.381 --> 00:08:26.867
that I couldn't get it, so I said,

00:08:26.867 --> 00:08:29.000
you know what, I'm going to
go in there, give 'em the

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.414
best damn joker they've
ever heard, and they're

00:08:31.414 --> 00:08:33.755
going to really regret the
fact that they can't hire me

00:08:33.755 --> 00:08:37.161
I was, I had this arrogance when I did it,

00:08:37.161 --> 00:08:38.927
which worked well for the part.

00:08:38.927 --> 00:08:41.591
I had a little trouble with
Sushi Girl, cos I read it

00:08:41.591 --> 00:08:43.772
and I thought wow, this
is awfully violent,

00:08:43.772 --> 00:08:48.254
he even tortures someone
with pliers to the teeth,

00:08:48.254 --> 00:08:50.076
and I've been married
to a dental hygienist

00:08:50.076 --> 00:08:53.345
for over 30 years, so may
the floss be with you,

00:08:53.345 --> 00:08:57.284
with her. But, er, I
thought, this is one of

00:08:57.284 --> 00:08:59.071
those movies that I'd like to see,

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:01.408
I don't know if I can handle being in it,

00:09:01.408 --> 00:09:05.154
so foolishly, I turned it
down, and my daughter said

00:09:05.154 --> 00:09:08.744
you know you're always
complaining about why don't I

00:09:08.744 --> 00:09:13.043
get parts like Paul Giamatti,
or why don't I get parts

00:09:13.043 --> 00:09:15.706
like Steve Buscemi, cos I
love those character parts,

00:09:15.706 --> 00:09:18.278
it doesn't have to be the
main role, just something

00:09:18.278 --> 00:09:19.845
interesting, and
supporting is fine with me,

00:09:19.845 --> 00:09:23.063
she said if you turn this
down, I don't want to

00:09:23.063 --> 00:09:26.167
ever hear you complain about
not getting character parts

00:09:26.167 --> 00:09:27.804
again, I thought, boy,
is she right. I wonder

00:09:27.804 --> 00:09:30.848
if it's too late, and I
called my agent, and said

00:09:30.848 --> 00:09:34.878
contact them, and say that I
want to meet with the director

00:09:34.878 --> 00:09:38.256
and fortunately they
hadn't cast that part,

00:09:38.256 --> 00:09:41.199
because as it turns out,
I mean, I didn't know

00:09:41.199 --> 00:09:43.282
anybody in the cast, except for Tony Todd,

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:45.696
and he has such gravitas,
he's such a great actor,

00:09:45.696 --> 00:09:48.444
and I was sort of his
sidekick. So I thought,

00:09:48.444 --> 00:09:52.611
well at least I know Tony,
and, erm, but it turned out

00:09:54.974 --> 00:09:56.799
to be, you know, one of
the most fulfilling roles

00:09:56.799 --> 00:10:00.916
I've ever played, and I didn't epect that.

00:10:00.916 --> 00:10:03.856
I once asked Lee Marvin for advice

00:10:03.856 --> 00:10:06.897
I meant career advice but
he thought for a moment,

00:10:06.897 --> 00:10:10.209
and he said, er, "never
stand when you can sit."

00:10:10.209 --> 00:10:13.810
and I still follow that
advice. There's a little

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:15.705
seat in the elevator, I
sat down on the way up.

00:10:15.705 --> 00:10:19.288
But, no, I usually take
advice by observing

00:10:20.585 --> 00:10:22.368
what other people do. You know?

00:10:22.368 --> 00:10:26.205
People say did you give the
young cast members advice,

00:10:26.205 --> 00:10:27.251
you know, in the new Star Wars film?

00:10:27.251 --> 00:10:30.355
I said absolutely not. They
are amazing, and in fact,

00:10:30.355 --> 00:10:35.078
if anyone should be getting
advice, it's me from them.

00:10:35.078 --> 00:10:37.989
- The first Star Wars in the
UK, from what I understand,

00:10:37.989 --> 00:10:40.104
was like a mini mutiny.
Everyone thought George Lucas

00:10:40.104 --> 00:10:44.063
was an idiot, nobody wanted
to be on the set, the actors

00:10:44.063 --> 00:10:46.623
were incredibly critical, some
of them, I'm just curious,

00:10:46.623 --> 00:10:50.634
sort of, to the extent you
observed it, kind of how

00:10:50.634 --> 00:10:52.384
you saw George Lucas.

00:10:53.269 --> 00:10:54.917
- [Mark] The attitude
towards the film when we

00:10:54.917 --> 00:10:55.870
were first making it?

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:58.807
- The first production, when
the part that was in the UK?

00:10:58.807 --> 00:11:01.252
Yeah, it was very badly,
so how did he kind of lead

00:11:01.252 --> 00:11:04.586
through that, how did
you lead through that?

00:11:04.586 --> 00:11:07.365
And what was your impression of him?

00:11:07.365 --> 00:11:10.422
- Well what I have to tell
you is that the crew that

00:11:10.422 --> 00:11:13.362
was making it, they were
all very professional

00:11:13.362 --> 00:11:18.023
but they all thought it
was, put kindly, "rubbish".

00:11:18.023 --> 00:11:22.190
They thought it was silly,
I mean, there's not a lot

00:11:23.404 --> 00:11:26.619
of things they can compare
it to, Doctor Who, maybe,

00:11:26.619 --> 00:11:28.775
there's a comic strip
called Dan Dare, the idiom

00:11:28.775 --> 00:11:32.942
was not really something that
was familiar to the British

00:11:33.822 --> 00:11:37.427
crew. So they were really
nice, and they liked all of us,

00:11:37.427 --> 00:11:40.295
but they just thought it
was ridiculous, they thought

00:11:40.295 --> 00:11:42.923
it would be a movie that would
be released only in matinee

00:11:42.923 --> 00:11:47.322
for children, in the daytime.
And I loved the British sense

00:11:47.322 --> 00:11:51.251
of humor, we had Kenny
the boom guy, you know,

00:11:51.251 --> 00:11:54.469
you'd be doing this
dialog, you know, you know,

00:11:54.469 --> 00:11:58.052
I want to go to Alderon
with you, you know,

00:11:59.345 --> 00:12:02.962
there are all these phrases
that were unusual to their ears,

00:12:02.962 --> 00:12:06.120
and it'd get picked up by the
crew, and you'd be walking

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:09.745
along the hallway and
you'd pass Kenny, he'd go,

00:12:09.745 --> 00:12:13.994
"Alderon, we're all going to
Alderon, mate, all of us..."

00:12:13.994 --> 00:12:16.475
(laughs)

00:12:16.475 --> 00:12:19.564
And you know, he'd always
be there, you'd do this

00:12:19.564 --> 00:12:22.399
really difficult dialog
and you'd look up at Kenny

00:12:22.399 --> 00:12:25.091
holding the boom, and he'd
always give you the eyeroll

00:12:25.091 --> 00:12:29.326
like, "ugh", (laughs), it
was all, and believe me,

00:12:29.326 --> 00:12:31.885
there was a typo one day
on one of the, when they

00:12:31.885 --> 00:12:36.459
were trying to type "Obe
wan kenobe", and they added

00:12:36.459 --> 00:12:40.626
an 'i' where they shouldn't,
and it came out "obe wonky"

00:12:41.709 --> 00:12:45.280
we didn't know what that
meant, but the crew never

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:46.697
let us forget it.

00:12:47.602 --> 00:12:50.472
(crowd laughs)

00:12:50.472 --> 00:12:51.432
(Kenny voice) "Obe Wonky,
alright, you alright?"

00:12:51.432 --> 00:12:55.182
But it, no. But they
were very kind about it.

00:12:56.929 --> 00:13:00.068
But I just thought, you
know, you have to believe

00:13:00.068 --> 00:13:01.311
in what you're doing.
I thought it was good.

00:13:01.311 --> 00:13:05.177
- Famously you almost did
all your stunts yourself

00:13:05.177 --> 00:13:06.889
- Except for a few, so I
noticed you shaped up since

00:13:06.889 --> 00:13:10.639
Sushi Girl. Are you
still into fencing stuff?

00:13:12.488 --> 00:13:17.267
- Well first of all, Bob
Anderson was the Olympic Champion

00:13:17.267 --> 00:13:21.434
and he made me look good,
because I, you choreograph it

00:13:22.397 --> 00:13:26.397
down to each and every
move. In those days, we'd

00:13:27.319 --> 00:13:31.486
choreograph the scene, and
then George looked at it,

00:13:33.459 --> 00:13:35.929
and he said "You can't take
your hand off the lightsabre"

00:13:35.929 --> 00:13:37.282
we said "why?" He said
"because it's heavy, it's like

00:13:37.282 --> 00:13:41.413
Excalibur," which really
limits the kind of movement

00:13:41.413 --> 00:13:45.709
you can make. You know,
flash forward a couple

00:13:45.709 --> 00:13:47.515
of decades and we're
watching Phantom Menace,

00:13:47.515 --> 00:13:49.951
and they're twirling
them around like batons.

00:13:49.951 --> 00:13:52.793
He changed his mind.
But we had to go back,

00:13:52.793 --> 00:13:55.161
and re-do the whole thing. So Bob Anderson

00:13:55.161 --> 00:13:58.058
I give full credit for
making me look like I was a

00:13:58.058 --> 00:14:01.806
competent sword fighter. The only stunts

00:14:01.806 --> 00:14:05.174
I wasn't allowed to do
because of the insurance,

00:14:05.174 --> 00:14:08.258
Colin Skeaping was my stunt double,

00:14:08.258 --> 00:14:11.476
and he went out the window
in Empire Strikes Back,

00:14:11.476 --> 00:14:14.128
and I think he did some of
the flips and some of the

00:14:14.128 --> 00:14:18.295
training sequences on Dagabah.
But I loved it at the time.

00:14:20.577 --> 00:14:24.389
When they wanted me to
get into shape for seven,

00:14:24.389 --> 00:14:28.038
they got me a trainer,
and I went to the gym

00:14:28.038 --> 00:14:32.853
and lost, I don't know, over,
like 42 pounds or something

00:14:32.853 --> 00:14:37.071
but, and you go on this
diet which is basically,

00:14:37.071 --> 00:14:41.238
if it tastes good, don't
eat it. There's no sugar,

00:14:43.343 --> 00:14:47.468
no dairy, no fun, it's
just, er, protein shakes,

00:14:47.468 --> 00:14:50.967
and lots and lots of...I've
eaten more vegetables

00:14:50.967 --> 00:14:54.519
in the last four years than I
have in my entire adult life

00:14:54.519 --> 00:14:56.971
But you know it's really
distressing when you figure out

00:14:56.971 --> 00:15:01.456
it's just diet and exercise?
Cos you want that magic thing

00:15:01.456 --> 00:15:04.901
you see the ads on TV,
don't change your diet,

00:15:04.901 --> 00:15:07.654
don't exercise, just take this pill,

00:15:07.654 --> 00:15:10.047
I'm writing down all the
information, my wife's saying

00:15:10.047 --> 00:15:11.977
you're not getting those
pills, whatever they are,

00:15:11.977 --> 00:15:15.428
and it's true, if you
work out, watch your diet,

00:15:15.428 --> 00:15:19.345
swim, it all pretty much
all falls away, so I'm

00:15:20.501 --> 00:15:24.723
very grateful to get
paid to get into shape.

00:15:24.723 --> 00:15:28.165
- Do you still train for fencing?

00:15:28.165 --> 00:15:31.665
- Hmmm. That's a loaded question. Perhaps.

00:15:34.754 --> 00:15:38.217
- Would you do a short dialog
between Luke and the Joker?

00:15:38.217 --> 00:15:41.050
(audience cheers)

00:15:47.694 --> 00:15:50.944
Hate to put you on the spot or anything

00:15:53.107 --> 00:15:56.878
- (Joker voice) Well, hmm.
Have to warm up to be Joker.

00:15:56.878 --> 00:16:01.045
Luke! It's me! The clown
prince of crime! And also...

00:16:03.133 --> 00:16:04.133
Your father!

00:16:05.767 --> 00:16:06.855
(Luke Skywalker voice)
Noo! That's not true!

00:16:06.855 --> 00:16:08.339
That's impossible!

00:16:08.339 --> 00:16:10.326
(Joker) Get used to it, kiddo (laughs)

00:16:10.326 --> 00:16:12.909
(crowd cheers)

00:16:19.027 --> 00:16:20.389
- Thank you so much guys for watching

00:16:20.389 --> 00:16:23.784
I made this video because
DarkClaude 4 asked me to

00:16:23.784 --> 00:16:25.843
If there's someone you want
to vote on for the next top 10

00:16:25.843 --> 00:16:29.165
check the link in the description
and go and have your say

00:16:29.165 --> 00:16:31.737
Finally wanted to give a quick
shoutout to Matthew Britt

00:16:31.737 --> 00:16:34.221
Matthew, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book

00:16:34.221 --> 00:16:36.248
"Your One Word", taking
that picture, posting it to

00:16:36.248 --> 00:16:39.041
Instagram, I really
appreciate the support,

00:16:39.041 --> 00:16:41.085
and I'm so glad you enjoyed the read.

00:16:41.085 --> 00:16:42.990
So thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:45.185
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:16:45.185 --> 00:16:47.745
And whatever your one word is. Much love.

00:16:47.745 --> 00:16:49.727
I'll see you soon.

00:16:49.727 --> 00:16:51.945
(swooshing noise.)

